  "content": {
    "id": "coordinate-inverse-distance-r36-1-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Calculate the values of $k$ if the distance between $(k,-1)$ and $(-3,k)$ is exactly $2\\sqrt{5}$ units.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$k=1$ or $-5$. Square the distance equation and factorise the resulting quadratic.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\sqrt{(-3-k)^2+(k-(-1))^2}&=2\\sqrt{5}\\\\(-3-k)^2+(k+1)^2&=20\\\\9+6k+k^2+k^2+2k+1&=20\\\\2k^2+8k+10&=20\\\\2k^2+8k-10&=0\\\\k^2+4k-5&=0\\end{align*}$$PSF: Product $=-5$, Sum $=4$, Factors $=5$ and $-1$.$$\\begin{align*}(k+5)(k-1)&=0\\\\k&=-5\\text{ or }1\\end{align*}$$Check $k=1$: $\\sqrt{(-4)^2+2^2}=\\sqrt{20}$. Check $k=-5$: $\\sqrt{2^2+(-4)^2}=\\sqrt{20}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": null
  },
